{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12650","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12650","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12650","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chan Sik","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Chan Sik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keon Myung","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Keon Myung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Leveraging Intermediate Representations of Time Series Foundation Models for Anomaly Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T04:10:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:21:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting anomalies in time series data is essential for the reliable operation of many real-world systems. Recently, time series foundation models (TSFMs) have emerged as a powerful tool for anomaly detection. However, existing methods typically rely on the final layer's representations of TSFMs, computing the anomaly score as a reconstruction or forecasting error via a task-specific head. Instead, we propose TimeRep, a novel anomaly detection approach that leverages the intermediate layer's representations of TSFMs, computing the anomaly score as the distance between these representations. Given a pre-trained TSFM, TimeRep selects the intermediate layer and patch-token position that yield the most informative representation. TimeRep forms a reference collection of intermediate representations from the training data and applies a core-set strategy to reduce its size while maintaining distributional coverage. During inference, TimeRep computes the anomaly score for incoming data by measuring the distance between its intermediate representations and those of the collection. To address concept drift, TimeRep integrates an adaptation mechanism that, at inference time, augments the collection exclusively with non-redundant intermediate representations from incoming data. We conducted extensive experiments on the UCR Anomaly Archive, which contains 250 univariate time series. TimeRep consistently outperforms a broad spectrum of state-of-the-art baselines, including non-DL, DL, and foundation model-based methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages,8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12650","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:45:20Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:45:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:45:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12585","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12585","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12585","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng-Jia","familyName":"Chu","name":"Chu, Meng-Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Z. D.","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Z. D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unidirectional and collective emission of integrated quantum emitters"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optics (physics.optics)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T02:23:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:16:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Unidirectional emission holds significant potential for advancing integrated photonics and quantum information technologies. However, the inherent randomness of spontaneous emission fundamentally makes its efficient realization rather challenging. To address this, here we develop a quantitative metric -- iso-frequency contour straightness and implement Fourier-transform analysis of radiation patterns to systematically evaluate directional quality of emission in photonic crystal (PhC) slabs. Through structural optimization, we demonstrate single-emitter radiation efficiency enhancement while maintaining low-loss unidirectional propagation. Furthermore, by strategically positioning multi-emitter arrays within PhC platforms, we simultaneously achieve scalable intensity amplification and superradiant emission via cooperative effects. This synergy of photonic band engineering and collective emitter coupling is able to realize unprecedented spatiotemporal coherence control in quantum emitter arrays."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12585","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:48Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niraj Kumar","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Niraj Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junjing","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Junjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Utpal","familyName":"Chatterjee","name":"Chatterjee, Utpal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Particle-hole symmetry in the pseudogap phase of moderately underdoped cuprate high temperature superconductors evidenced from joint density of states analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T01:50:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:15:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In conventional superconductors, the energy scale associated with the superfluid stiffness is much larger compared to the pairing energy and hence, the superconducting transition temperature (Tc) is entirely dictated by the superconducting (SC) energy gap. The phase rigidity of the SC condensate in unconventional superconductors, on the other hand, can be low enough to enable destruction of superconductivity via phase incoherence and persistence of an energy gap even at the absence of macroscopic superconductivity above Tc. This is considered a possible mechanism of the pseudogap (PG) state of cuprate high temperature superconductors (HTSCs). We have investigated the electronic energy (ω) and momentum-separation vector (q) dependence of the joint density of states (JDOS), derived from the autocorrelated Angle Resolved Photoemission Spectroscopy (ARPES) data, from moderately underdoped Bi2Sr2CaCu2O8+δHTSC samples at temperatures below and above Tc. We found that q-space structure of the constant ω JDOS intensity maps and the dispersions of the JDOS peaks are essentially the same both below and above Tc. Furthermore, the dispersions of the JDOS peaks above Tc are particle-hole symmetric. These observations evince similarity between the nature of the energy gap below and above Tc, which supports preformed pairing scenario for the PG state at least in the moderately underdoped regime."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:25Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12562","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12562","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12562","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhefei","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Zhefei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shangke","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Shangke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengxiang","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Pengxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Donglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Online Residual Refinement via Koopman-Guided Dynamics Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-16T01:37:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:14:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Imitation learning (IL) enables efficient skill acquisition from demonstrations but often struggles with long-horizon tasks and high-precision control due to compounding errors. Residual policy learning offers a promising, model-agnostic solution by refining a base policy through closed-loop corrections. However, existing approaches primarily focus on local corrections to the base policy, lacking a global understanding of state evolution, which limits robustness and generalization to unseen scenarios. To address this, we propose incorporating global dynamics modeling to guide residual policy updates. Specifically, we leverage Koopman operator theory to impose linear time-invariant structure in a learned latent space, enabling reliable state transitions and improved extrapolation for long-horizon prediction and unseen environments. We introduce KORR (Koopman-guided Online Residual Refinement), a simple yet effective framework that conditions residual corrections on Koopman-predicted latent states, enabling globally informed and stable action refinement. We evaluate KORR on long-horizon, fine-grained robotic furniture assembly tasks under various perturbations. Results demonstrate consistent gains in performance, robustness, and generalization over strong baselines. Our findings further highlight the potential of Koopman-based modeling to bridge modern learning methods with classical control theory."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12562","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:43:17Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:43:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:43:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12494","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12494","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12494","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naifeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Naifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sophia","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Sophia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Franz","familyName":"Franchetti","name":"Franchetti, Franz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Closing the Performance Gap for Cryptographic Kernels Between CPUs and Specialized Hardware"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T22:35:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:11:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Specialized hardware like application-specific integrated circuits (ASICs) remains the primary accelerator type for cryptographic kernels based on large integer arithmetic. Prior work has shown that commodity and server-class GPUs can achieve near-ASIC performance for these workloads. However, achieving comparable performance on CPUs remains an open challenge. This work investigates the following question: How can we narrow the performance gap between CPUs and specialized hardware for key cryptographic kernels like basic linear algebra subprograms (BLAS) operations and the number theoretic transform (NTT)?\n To this end, we develop an optimized scalar implementation of these kernels for x86 CPUs at the per-core level. We utilize SIMD instructions (specifically AVX2 and AVX-512) to further improve performance, achieving an average speedup of 38 times and 62 times over state-of-the-art CPU baselines for NTTs and BLAS operations, respectively. To narrow the gap further, we propose a small AVX-512 extension, dubbed multi-word extension (MQX), which delivers substantial speedup with only three new instructions and minimal proposed hardware modifications. MQX cuts the slowdown relative to ASICs to as low as 35 times on a single CPU core. Finally, we perform a roofline analysis to evaluate the peak performance achievable with MQX when scaled across an entire multi-core CPU. Our results show that, with MQX, top-tier server-grade CPUs can approach the performance of state-of-the-art ASICs for cryptographic workloads."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the IEEE/ACM International Symposium on Microarchitecture (MICRO), 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12494","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:42Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:41:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sriram","familyName":"Nagaraj","name":"Nagaraj, Sriram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishakh","familyName":"Hari","name":"Hari, Vishakh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Nonlocal Neural Tangent Kernels via Parameter-Space Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"90C56","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T21:23:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:09:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Neural Tangent Kernel (NTK) framework has provided deep insights into the training dynamics of neural networks under gradient flow. However, it relies on the assumption that the network is differentiable with respect to its parameters, an assumption that breaks down when considering non-smooth target functions or parameterized models exhibiting non-differentiable behavior. In this work, we propose a Nonlocal Neural Tangent Kernel (NNTK) that replaces the local gradient with a nonlocal interaction-based approximation in parameter space. Nonlocal gradients are known to exist for a wider class of functions than the standard gradient. This allows NTK theory to be extended to nonsmooth functions, stochastic estimators, and broader families of models. We explore both fixed-kernel and attention-based formulations of this nonlocal operator. We illustrate the new formulation with numerical studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:41:05Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:41:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:41:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12399","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12399","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12399","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"LHCb collaboration","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unveiling the shape of the $^{20}$Ne nucleus by measuring the flow coefficients with cumulants in PbNe and PbAr collisions at $\\sqrt{s_{NN}} = 70.9$ GeV"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T19:46:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:06:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The anisotropic flow coefficients $v_n$ quantify the collective medium response to the initial spatial anisotropy of the overlapping region in ion collisions and serve as sensitive probes of both the medium properties and shape of nuclear initial states. In this analysis, the $v_2$ and $v_3$ parameters of prompt charged particles are measured using the multiparticle cumulant method in fixed-target PbNe and PbAr collisions at $\\sqrt{s_{NN}} = 70.9$ GeV, collected by LHCb using the SMOG2 gas-target system during the 2024 LHC lead-beam run. The cumulant method is first validated using 2018 PbPb collision data, successfully reproducing previous measurements obtained via the two-particle correlation method. Results for the fixed-target collisions are then presented, showing a significantly larger value of the elliptic flow coefficient $v_2$ in central PbNe with respect to PbAr collisions. This is qualitatively consistent with 3+1D hydrodynamic predictions including ab-initio descriptions of the nuclear structure. The results provide the first experimental confirmation of the distinctive bowling-pin shape of the ground-state $^{20}$Ne nucleus, validating at the same time the hydrodynamic description of the hot medium formed in high-energy collisions involving light ions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"All figures and tables, along with machine-readable versions and any supplementary material and additional information, are available at https://lbfence.cern.ch/alcm/public/analysis/full-details/5730/ (LHCb public pages)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12399","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:39:32Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:39:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:39:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12358","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12358","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12358","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua A.","familyName":"Vita","name":"Vita, Joshua A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit","familyName":"Samanta","name":"Samanta, Amit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincenzo","familyName":"Lordi","name":"Lordi, Vincenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unsupervised Atomic Data Mining via Multi-Kernel Graph Autoencoders for Machine Learning Force Fields"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:41:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:04:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Constructing a chemically diverse dataset while avoiding sampling bias is critical to training efficient and generalizable force fields. However, in computational chemistry and materials science, many common dataset generation techniques are prone to oversampling regions of the potential energy surface. Furthermore, these regions can be difficult to identify and isolate from each other or may not align well with human intuition, making it challenging to systematically remove bias in the dataset. While traditional clustering and pruning (down-sampling) approaches can be useful for this, they can often lead to information loss or a failure to properly identify distinct regions of the potential energy surface due to difficulties associated with the high dimensionality of atomic descriptors. In this work, we introduce the Multi-kernel Edge Attention-based Graph Autoencoder (MEAGraph) model, an unsupervised approach for analyzing atomic datasets. MEAGraph combines multiple linear kernel transformations with attention-based message passing to capture geometric sensitivity and enable effective dataset pruning without relying on labels or extensive training. Demonstrated applications on niobium, tantalum, and iron datasets show that MEAGraph efficiently groups similar atomic environments, allowing for the use of basic pruning techniques for removing sampling bias. This approach provides an effective method for representation learning and clustering that can be used for data analysis, outlier detection, and dataset optimization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12358","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:37Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12355","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12355","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12355","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergi","familyName":"Mas-Mendoza","name":"Mas-Mendoza, Sergi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard D. P.","familyName":"East","name":"East, Richard D. P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michele","familyName":"Filippone","name":"Filippone, Michele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adolfo G.","familyName":"Grushin","name":"Grushin, Adolfo G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A graphical diagnostic of topological order using ZX calculus"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:34:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:04:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Establishing a universal diagnostic of topological order remains an open theoretical challenge. In particular, diagnosing long-range entanglement through the entropic area law suffers from spurious contributions, failing to unambiguously identify topological order. Here we devise a protocol based on the ZX calculus, a graphical tensor network, to determine the topological order of a state circumventing entropy calculations. The protocol takes as input real-space bipartitions of a state and returns a ZX contour diagram, $\\mathcal{D}_{\\partial A}$, displaying long-range graph connectivity only for long-range entangled states. We validate the protocol by showing that the contour diagrams of the toric and color codes are equivalent except for the number of non-local nodes, which differentiates their topological order. The number of these nodes is robust to the choice of the boundary and ground-state superposition, and they are absent for trivial states, even those with spurious entropy contributions. Our results single out ZX calculus as a tool to detect topological long-range entanglement by leveraging the advantages of diagrammatic reasoning against entropic diagnostics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 26 pages of supplemental material"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12355","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:32Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12353","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12353","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12353","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anthony","familyName":"Miyaguchi","name":"Miyaguchi, Anthony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chandrasekaran","familyName":"Maruthaiyannan","name":"Maruthaiyannan, Chandrasekaran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles R.","familyName":"Clark","name":"Clark, Charles R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DS@GT AnimalCLEF: Triplet Learning over ViT Manifolds with Nearest Neighbor Classification for Animal Re-identification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:31:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:04:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper details the DS@GT team's entry for the AnimalCLEF 2025 re-identification challenge. Our key finding is that the effectiveness of post-hoc metric learning is highly contingent on the initial quality and domain-specificity of the backbone embeddings. We compare a general-purpose model (DINOv2) with a domain-specific model (MegaDescriptor) as a backbone. A K-Nearest Neighbor classifier with robust thresholding then identifies known individuals or flags new ones. While a triplet-learning projection head improved the performance of the specialized MegaDescriptor model by 0.13 points, it yielded minimal gains (0.03) for the general-purpose DINOv2 on averaged BAKS and BAUS. We demonstrate that the general-purpose manifold is more difficult to reshape for fine-grained tasks, as evidenced by stagnant validation loss and qualitative visualizations. This work highlights the critical limitations of refining general-purpose features for specialized, limited-data re-ID tasks and underscores the importance of domain-specific pre-training. The implementation for this work is publicly available at github.com/dsgt-arc/animalclef-2025."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CLEF 2025 working notes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12353","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:38:30Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:38:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:38:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12320","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12320","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12320","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Glenn","familyName":"Wagner","name":"Wagner, Glenn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yves H.","familyName":"Kwan","name":"Kwan, Yves H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nick","familyName":"Bultinck","name":"Bultinck, Nick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steven H.","familyName":"Simon","name":"Simon, Steven H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"S. A.","familyName":"Parameswaran","name":"Parameswaran, S. A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Putting a new spin on the incommensurate Kekulé spiral: from spin-valley locking and collective modes to fermiology and implications for superconductivity"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Strongly Correlated Electrons (cond-mat.str-el)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Superconductivity (cond-mat.supr-con)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T18:00:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We revisit the global phase diagram of magic-angle twisted bilayer and [symmetric] trilayer graphene (MA-TBG/TSTG) in light of recent scanning tunneling microscopy (STM) measurements on these materials. These experiments both confirmed the importance of strain in stabilizing the predicted incommensurate Kekulé spiral (IKS) order near filling $|ν|=2$ of the weakly dispersive central bands in both systems, and suggested a key role for electron-phonon couplings and short-range Coulomb interactions in selecting between various competing orders at low strain in MA-TBG. Here, we show that such interactions $\\textit{also}$ play a crucial role in selecting the spin structure of the strain-stabilized IKS state. This in turn influences the visibility of the IKS order in STM in a manner that allows us to infer their relative importance. We use this insight in conjunction with various other pieces of experimental data to build a more complete picture of the phase diagram, focusing on the spectrum of low-lying collective modes and the nature of the doped Fermi surfaces. We explore the broad phenomenological implications of these results for superconductivity."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 + 9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12320","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:37:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12295","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12295","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12295","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Tavernor","name":"Tavernor, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily Mower","familyName":"Provost","name":"Provost, Emily Mower","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Similar than Dissimilar: Modeling Annotators for Cross-Corpus Speech Emotion Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T15:52:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech emotion recognition systems often predict a consensus value generated from the ratings of multiple annotators. However, these models have limited ability to predict the annotation of any one person. Alternatively, models can learn to predict the annotations of all annotators. Adapting such models to new annotators is difficult as new annotators must individually provide sufficient labeled training data. We propose to leverage inter-annotator similarity by using a model pre-trained on a large annotator population to identify a similar, previously seen annotator. Given a new, previously unseen, annotator and limited enrollment data, we can make predictions for a similar annotator, enabling off-the-shelf annotation of unseen data in target datasets, providing a mechanism for extremely low-cost personalization. We demonstrate our approach significantly outperforms other off-the-shelf approaches, paving the way for lightweight emotion adaptation, practical for real-world deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"\\copyright 20XX IEEE. Personal use of this material is permitted. Permission from IEEE must be obtained for all other uses, in any current or future media, including reprinting/republishing this material for advertising or promotional purposes, creating new collective works, for resale or redistribution to servers or lists, or reuse of any copyrighted component of this work in other works"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12295","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:37:10Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:37:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:37:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12272","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12272","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12272","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasuhiro","familyName":"Takei","name":"Takei, Yasuhiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoritaka","familyName":"Iwata","name":"Iwata, Yoritaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dynamical symmetry breaking described by cubic nonlinear Klein-Gordon equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Theory (hep-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Adaptation and Self-Organizing Systems (nlin.AO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chaotic Dynamics (nlin.CD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"Primary 81R40, 81Q05, Secondary 65M70, 65L06","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T01:41:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The dynamical symmetry breaking associated with the existence and non-existence of breather solutions is studied. Here, nonlinear hyperbolic evolution equations are calculated using a high-precision numerical scheme. %%% First, for clarifying the dynamical symmetry breaking, it is necessary to use a sufficiently high-precision scheme in the time-dependent framework. Second, the error of numerical calculations is generally more easily accumulated for calculating hyperbolic equations rather than parabolic equations. Third, numerical calculations become easily unstable for nonlinear cases. Our strategy for the high-precision and stable scheme is to implement the implicit Runge-Kutta method for time, and the Fourier spectral decomposition for space. %%% In this paper, focusing on the breather solutions, the relationship between the velocity, mass, and the amplitude of the perturbation is clarified. As a result, the conditions for transitioning from one state to another are clarified."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"to be published in Springer Proceedings in Mathematics and Statistics (SPMS)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12272","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:39Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12244","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12244","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12244","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min","familyName":"Xian","name":"Xian, Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yalei","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Yalei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shoukun","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Shoukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Stempien","name":"Stempien, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RU-Net for Automatic Characterization of TRISO Fuel Cross Sections"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-10T23:04:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:01:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"During irradiation, phenomena such as kernel swelling and buffer densification may impact the performance of tristructural isotropic (TRISO) particle fuel. Post-irradiation microscopy is often used to identify these irradiation-induced morphologic changes. However, each fuel compact generally contains thousands of TRISO particles. Manually performing the work to get statistical information on these phenomena is cumbersome and subjective. To reduce the subjectivity inherent in that process and to accelerate data analysis, we used convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to automatically segment cross-sectional images of microscopic TRISO layers. CNNs are a class of machine-learning algorithms specifically designed for processing structured grid data. They have gained popularity in recent years due to their remarkable performance in various computer vision tasks, including image classification, object detection, and image segmentation. In this research, we generated a large irradiated TRISO layer dataset with more than 2,000 microscopic images of cross-sectional TRISO particles and the corresponding annotated images. Based on these annotated images, we used different CNNs to automatically segment different TRISO layers. These CNNs include RU-Net (developed in this study), as well as three existing architectures: U-Net, Residual Network (ResNet), and Attention U-Net. The preliminary results show that the model based on RU-Net performs best in terms of Intersection over Union (IoU). Using CNN models, we can expedite the analysis of TRISO particle cross sections, significantly reducing the manual labor involved and improving the objectivity of the segmentation results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12244","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:36:00Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:36:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:36:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12232","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12232","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12232","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianmarco","familyName":"Accordi","name":"Accordi, Gianmarco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Domke","name":"Domke, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Theresa","familyName":"Pollinger","name":"Pollinger, Theresa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Davide","familyName":"Gadioli","name":"Gadioli, Davide","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gianluca","familyName":"Palermo","name":"Palermo, Gianluca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards High-Performance and Portable Molecular Docking on CPUs through Vectorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-08T13:15:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent trends in the HPC field have introduced new CPU architectures with improved vectorization capabilities that require optimization to achieve peak performance and thus pose challenges for performance portability. The deployment of high-performing scientific applications for CPUs requires adapting the codebase and optimizing for performance. Evaluating these applications provides insights into the complex interactions between code, compilers, and hardware. We evaluate compiler auto-vectorization and explicit vectorization to achieve performance portability across modern CPUs with long vectors. We select a molecular docking application as a case study, as it represents computational patterns commonly found across HPC workloads. We report insights into the technical challenges, architectural trends, and optimization strategies relevant to the future development of scientific applications for HPC. Our results show which code transformations enable portable auto-vectorization, reaching performance similar to explicit vectorization. Experimental data confirms that x86 CPUs typically achieve higher execution performance than ARM CPUs, primarily due to their wider vectorization units. However, ARM architectures demonstrate competitive energy consumption and cost-effectiveness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12232","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-17T01:35:44Z","registered":"2025-09-17T01:35:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T01:35:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12210","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.12210","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.12210","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["NTT DATA Corporation"],"givenName":"Masaru","familyName":"Dobashi","name":"Dobashi, Masaru","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["NTT DATA Corporation"],"givenName":"Kohei","familyName":"Toshimitsu","name":"Toshimitsu, Kohei","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Koshizuka Laboratory"],"givenName":"Hirotsugu","familyName":"Seike","name":"Seike, Hirotsugu","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["NTT DATA Corporation"],"givenName":"Miki","familyName":"Kanno","name":"Kanno, Miki","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Koshizuka Laboratory"],"givenName":"Genki","familyName":"Horie","name":"Horie, Genki","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["Koshizuka Laboratory"],"givenName":"Noboru","familyName":"Koshizuka","name":"Koshizuka, Noboru","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Proposal for High-Level Architectural Model Capable of Expressing Various Data Collaboration Platform and Data Space Concepts"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-28T09:50:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes \"Data Space High-Level Architecture Model\" (DS-HLAM) for expressing diverse data collaboration platforms across regional implementations. The framework introduces mathematically rigorous definitions with success conditions formalized through finite state automata theory, enabling interoperability while preserving digital sovereignty requirements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Comments: 16 pages, 3 figures, uses amsmath, amssymb, amsfonts, graphicx, authblk"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.12210","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"